  ===================================================================
  SUPPLY CHAIN GATE - ACCEPTED FINDINGS
  ===================================================================
  This file exists so that the supply chain scan can examine every
  dependency the build executes, in every scope, at the configured
  threshold. It is the narrow alternative to excluding a whole scope
  from the scan: excluding test scoped artifacts would have hidden
  every finding in every test scoped artifact, whereas each rule here
  names one artifact and enumerates the individual findings accepted
  against it, so any other finding in the same artifact still fails
  the build.

  Three properties keep this file from becoming a silent bypass.

  First, every rule below is scoped to one file inside one artifact
  and lists its accepted findings individually. The scope is a file
  path rather than a coordinate on purpose: these findings are
  reported against a relocated copy of another library carried inside
  a single archive, and the path is the only identifier that names
  both the archive that carries the copy and the copy itself. A rule
  written against the coordinate alone would also match that library
  wherever else it appeared, including as a direct dependency, which
  is exactly what must not be suppressed. A rule with no
  vulnerability element is not permitted here. A rule matching a
  family of artifacts is permitted only where the identifier being
  matched is itself attached to the whole family by the data source
  rather than to one module, and only when the vulnerable code has been
  shown to be absent from every module of that family on the graph; the
  rule is then bounded by an exact version and by the individual
  identifier, so it accepts one dated finding and not a class of them.

  Second, the plugin is configured to fail the build on an unused
  suppression rule. The moment an accepted finding stops being
  reported - because a fixed release reaches the graph - this file
  stops matching and the build fails until the obsolete rule is
  deleted. An accepted finding therefore cannot outlive the reason it
  was accepted.

  Third, every rule carries an expiry date. On that date the rule
  stops applying, the finding is reported again, and the build fails
  until the acceptance has been re-examined against whatever releases
  exist by then. Acceptance here is always a dated decision, never a
  standing exemption.
  ===================================================================
-->
<suppressions xmlns="https://jeremylong.github.io/DependencyCheck/dependency-suppression.1.3.xsd">

  <!--
    Container engine client transport, reached only through the
    container testing library and therefore only ever test scoped.

    Why the finding cannot be remediated by a version change. This
    artifact exists specifically to carry relocated copies of its own
    dependencies so that it has none of its own, and one of those
    copies is the HTTP core library these two findings are reported
    against. A relocated class cannot be replaced by managing the
    coordinate it was copied from, because the coordinate is no longer
    on the graph. The artifact itself therefore has to move, and it
    has: this build pins the whole client family to the newest release
    of the line, which relocates a materially newer HTTP core than the
    version the library would otherwise have selected, and that move
    cleared every other finding the older relocation carried.

    Why that move is not sufficient. Both findings affect the entire
    5.0 to 5.4.2 range inclusive, together with the 5.5 alpha and beta
    pre-releases. Every published release of this transport relocates
    a version inside that range, the newest included, so no available
    version clears these two. The dates settle it: the first fixed
    release of the relocated library was published three months after
    the newest release of this transport, so there has not yet been a
    transport release that could carry the fix. The next major line of
    the container testing library was checked as well and depends on
    exactly the same client release, so upgrading the testing library
    would not change the outcome either. The testing library also
    offers no alternative transport: it selects this implementation by
    direct class reference, with no fallback, and rejects any other
    transport name outright, and the non-relocating transport does not
    contain the class it references, so substitution is not available.

    Why the rule is scoped to a path. The finding is reported against
    the relocated copy, whose coordinate is that of the library it was
    copied from, not that of the archive carrying it. Scoping by that
    coordinate would suppress the same findings anywhere that library
    appeared, including as a direct dependency of the application. The
    path names the copy inside this one archive and nothing else.

    Why the path matches two modules. The relocated library is split
    into a core module and a companion module for the second protocol
    version, and the archive carries a copy of both. One finding is
    against the first protocol version's message parser and the other
    against the second version's header compression decoder, but the
    published product identifier is the same for both modules, so each
    finding is reported twice, once per module. The path therefore
    names those two modules explicitly and no others: the archive also
    carries relocated copies of the client library and of its own
    source module, and findings against either of those would still
    fail the build.

    Why the residual exposure is acceptable. Both findings are
    uncontrolled resource consumption reachable by a remote attacker
    that sends the affected parser a hostile message: an HTTP/1.1
    message with an excessive number or length of headers, or an
    oversized HTTP/2 compressed header block delivered before the
    settings acknowledgement applies the header list size limit. Both
    therefore require the affected code to be parsing input from an
    untrusted peer. Here the code is an outbound client that speaks to
    the container engine daemon on the local machine during a test
    run, so the only peer it parses responses from is that daemon.
    The artifact is test scoped, so it is absent from the packaged
    application and from every deployable image the build produces.

    Both findings remain reported in the scan output; this rule
    records them as accepted, it does not remove them from the report.
  -->
  <suppress until="2027-02-01Z">
    <notes><![CDATA[
      Relocated HTTP core copy inside the test scoped container engine
      transport. Every published release of this transport relocates a
      version inside the affected range, and the transport cannot be
      substituted because the container testing library selects it by
      direct class reference and the non-relocating transport does not
      contain that class. Test scope only, so absent from the packaged
      application, and the affected parsers only ever read responses
      from the container engine daemon on the local machine.
    ]]></notes>
    <filePath regex="true">^.*[/\\]docker-java-transport-zerodep-[0-9][^/\\]*\.jar[/\\].*[/\\]org\.apache\.httpcomponents\.core5[/\\]httpcore5(-h2)?[/\\]pom\.xml$</filePath>
    <cve>CVE-2026-54399</cve>
    <cve>CVE-2026-54428</cve>
  </suppress>

  <!--
    Asynchronous transport of the cloud client stack, reached through the
    asynchronous HTTP client the object-storage and queue clients use.
    This one is compile scoped, so unlike the rule above it is present in
    the packaged application, and it is accepted on the narrower ground
    that the vulnerable code is not merely unreachable but absent.

    Why the finding does not describe code this build carries. The
    advisory is against the HTTP/3 frame codec, which buffers incoming
    data for reserved frame types up to the length declared on the wire
    and so lets a peer drive unbounded allocation. That codec is not part
    of the 4.1 line at all: it ships in a separate incubating artifact
    that this build does not depend on, directly or transitively. Every
    artifact of this family on the graph was examined and none contains a
    single class entry naming that protocol version or its underlying
    transport protocol, so there is no code path to the defect, reachable
    or otherwise. The finding is a product level match against the
    family's published identifier, which the scanner attaches to every
    module of the family at the affected version rather than to the one
    module that carries the codec.

    Why the finding cannot be remediated by a version change. The fix is
    published only on the next minor line. Moving to it is a framework
    wide major move for this build: the line is selected by the managed
    dependency set of the application framework, the cloud client stack
    resolves its own transport from the same line, and nothing else on
    the graph is prepared for the change. Pinning one module of the
    family across lines is worse than the finding: the family is
    published and tested as a unit and mixing lines inside it is
    explicitly unsupported.

    Why this rule names the family and not one module, unlike the rule
    above. The identifier being matched is itself a product level one:
    the scanner attaches the family's published identifier to every
    module of the family at the affected version, so the module the
    finding surfaces against is whichever module is examined and not
    suppressed. Naming one module was tried and simply moved the finding
    to the next module of the same family, which is the shape of a
    product level match rather than of a defect in a particular module.
    The rule therefore names the family, and the acceptance is bounded by
    two other things instead: the exact pinned version, and the single
    identifier listed below. Every artifact of the family on the graph
    was examined for the codec named by the advisory and none carries a
    class entry for it, so widening the artifact match does not widen
    what is being accepted - it names the same absence in every module
    that the identifier is attached to. The moment the pin moves, this
    rule matches nothing, the plugin fails the build on the unused rule,
    and the acceptance has to be re-made against whatever the new
    version reports.

    The finding remains reported in the scan output; this rule records it
    as accepted, it does not remove it from the report.
  -->
  <suppress until="2027-02-01Z">
    <notes><![CDATA[
      HTTP/3 frame codec denial of service reported against the
      asynchronous transport module of the network framework family. The
      codec named by the advisory ships in a separate incubating artifact
      that is not on this graph in any scope, and no artifact of the
      family carries a class entry for that protocol version, so the
      vulnerable code is absent rather than unreachable. The fix exists
      only on the next minor line, which the application framework's
      managed dependency set does not select and which cannot be adopted
      for one module of the family alone.
    ]]></notes>
    <packageUrl regex="true">^pkg:maven/io\.netty/netty-[^@]*@4\.1\.136\.Final$</packageUrl>
    <cve>CVE-2026-56816</cve>
  </suppress>

</suppressions>
